To get a list of all installed applications on Windows, you can use PowerShell for similar functionality to `system_profiler` in macOS. The following code snippet will retrieve the list of installed applications and export it to a JSON file:

```powershell
Get-WmiObject -Class Win32_Product | Select-Object Name, Version | ConvertTo-Json | Out-File installed_apps.json
```

You can then parse this JSON file in Rust. Here’s a rough example of how to do that in Rust:

```rust
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::fs;

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Application {
    name: String,
    version: String,
}

fn main() {
    let data = fs::read_to_string("installed_apps.json").unwrap();
    let apps: Vec<Application> = serde_json::from_str(&data).unwrap();
    for app in apps {
        println!("{} - {}", app.name, app.version);
    }
}
```

This Rust code will read a JSON file containing the list of installed applications and print out their names and versions. Make sure you have the `serde` and `serde_json` crates added to your `Cargo.toml`.

